    <dashboard name='What Drives Conversion? A Look at Channel and Campaign Performance' type='storyboard'>
      <layout-options>
        <title>
          <formatted-text>
            <run fontalignment='1'>&lt;Sheet Name&gt;</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </title>
      </layout-options>
      <style>
        <style-rule element='story-point-caption'>
          <format attr='width' value='902' />
          <format attr='background-color' value='#679ad8' />
          <format attr='font-size' value='12' />
        </style-rule>
      </style>
      <size maxheight='964' maxwidth='1016' minheight='964' minwidth='1016' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='2' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone h='98340' id='1' param='vert' removable='false' type-v2='layout-flow' w='98426' x='787' y='830'>
            <zone h='3423' id='3' type-v2='title' w='98426' x='787' y='830' />
            <zone fixed-size='157' h='16286' id='4' is-fixed='true' paired-zone-id='5' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard-nav' w='98426' x='787' y='4253' />
            <zone h='78631' id='5' paired-zone-id='4' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard' w='98426' x='787' y='20539'>
              <flipboard active-id='9' nav-type='caption' show-nav-arrows='true'>
                <story-points>
                  <story-point caption='Across this campaign period, the business invested $40.01M in digital marketing, generating 198K website visits and an average click-through rate of 15.48%. Overall conversion rate landed at 10.44%. The natural question for any marketer looking at numbers like these: what&apos;s actually driving that conversion: the channel, the campaign type, or something else entirely?' captured-sheet='Total Ad Spend' id='3'>
                    <capturedDeltas>
                      <worksheet name='Total Ad Spend'>
                        <selection-collection />
                      </worksheet>
                    </capturedDeltas>
                  </story-point>
                  <story-point caption='Breaking conversion rate out by channel, the answer starts to take shape or rather, doesn&apos;t. Social Media leads at 10.66%, Referral trails at 10.31%, with Email, PPC, and SEO clustered in between. The spread across all five channels is just 0.35 percentage points on $40M of spend. Channel alone isn&apos;t the driver of conversion here.' captured-sheet='Conversion Rate by Channel' id='5' />
                  <story-point caption='Same test, different variable: does funnel stage move the needle? Awareness, Consideration, Conversion, and Retention campaigns convert within 0.19 points of each other (10.32% - 10.51%). Even campaigns purpose-built to convert don&apos;t outperform top-of-funnel Awareness campaigns. Campaign type isn&apos;t the driver either.' captured-sheet='Conversion Rate by Campaign Type' id='6' />
                  <story-point caption='If channel and campaign type don&apos;t explain conversion, engagement might. Email Opens by Campaign Type shows real separation: Conversion-stage campaigns hit 19,707 opens, while Retention campaigns dropped to 18,575 over a thousand opens apart. That&apos;s a meaningfully larger swing than anything conversion rate shows across this entire dashboard. Engagement metrics, not channel or funnel stage, carry the signal in this data.' captured-sheet='Email Open by Campaign Type' id='7' />
                  <story-point caption='One more layer worth adding: spend wasn&apos;t distributed evenly, even though results were. Referral received the highest investment ($8.65M) but posted the lowest conversion rate. Social Media, the lowest-spend channel at $7.54M, converted best. So while channel doesn&apos;t drive conversion rate, it clearly drives cost-efficiency and that&apos;s a different, more actionable lever.' captured-sheet='Ad Spend by Channel' id='8' />
                  <story-point caption='The data answers its own question: channel and campaign type don&apos;t meaningfully drive conversion rate - engagement does, and spend efficiency does. The next move isn&apos;t picking a &apos;better&apos; channel, it&apos;s optimizing for opens and cost-per-outcome instead of chasing a conversion gap that barely exists.' captured-sheet='Cost per Acquisition' id='9'>
                    <currentDeltas />
                    <capturedDeltas>
                      <worksheet name='Cost per Acquisition'>
                        <selection-collection>
                          <tuple-selection>
                            <tuple-reference>
                              <tuple-descriptor>
                                <pane-descriptor>
                                  <x-fields />
                                  <y-fields />
                                </pane-descriptor>
                                <columns>
                                  <field>[federated.0ogdpo50v5inrf153rczm0aot00k].[usr:Calculation_0517917549735942:qk]</field>
                                </columns>
                              </tuple-descriptor>
                              <tuple>
                                <value>5705.5845184523541</value>
                              </tuple>
                            </tuple-reference>
                          </tuple-selection>
                        </selection-collection>
                      </worksheet>
                    </capturedDeltas>
                  </story-point>
                </story-points>
              </flipboard>
            </zone>
          </zone>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='margin' value='8' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
      </zones>
      <simple-id uuid='{3FF9994D-46E7-48EB-ACCE-38407656B239}' />
    </dashboard>
